## Default behavior (ripgrep 15.2.0 / current master)

The CLI's default is `MmapMode::Auto` (`#[default]` in `crates/core/flags/lowargs.rs:625-640`). In `crates/core/flags/hiargs.rs:222-249`, that becomes:

```rust
let maybe = unsafe { grep::searcher::MmapChoice::auto() };
let never = grep::searcher::MmapChoice::never();
match low.mmap {
    MmapMode::Auto => {
        if paths.paths.len() <= 10
            && paths.paths.iter().all(|p| p.is_file())
        {
            // If we're only searching a few paths and all of them
            // are files, then memory maps are probably faster.
            maybe
        } else {
            never
        }
    }
    MmapMode::AlwaysTryMmap => maybe,
    MmapMode::Never => never,
}
```

So **by default ripgrep enables memory maps only when it was given at most 10 paths and every one of those paths is a regular file** — e.g. `rg foo a.txt b.txt`. Things like `rg foo` (implicit `./`), `rg foo src/`, a glob expanding to 11+ files, or any directory/non-file argument disable mmap. `Path::is_file()` also excludes stdin (`-`), devices, etc.

## Additional restrictions even when enabled

- **macOS:** `MmapChoice::open()` hard-returns `None` for `cfg!(target_os = "macos")`, even under `--mmap` (`crates/searcher/src/searcher/mmap.rs:65-82`).
- **Only real files:** mmap is attempted only in `search_file_maybe_path`, on an already-open `File` (`crates/searcher/src/searcher/mod.rs:689`). Streams/stdin/preprocessor output use the reader path and are never mmap'd.
- If `Mmap::map` fails, ripgrep silently falls back to normal reads.
- The library `MmapChoice::default()` is `Never`; it is the CLI that raises it to `Auto`/`AlwaysTryMmap`.

## Flags that override it

| Flag | Effect |
|---|---|
| `--mmap` | Sets `MmapMode::AlwaysTryMmap` — try mmap for every eligible file, regardless of the ≤10/regular-file heuristic. |
| `--no-mmap` | Sets `MmapMode::Never` — never use mmap, including the multiline "read whole file to heap" path. |

Both are plain switches on the same flag (`name_negated` is `no-mmap`), and **the last one on the command line wins** (`crates/core/flags/defs.rs:4060-4101`). They can also come from `RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH`.

`--mmap` does *not* override the macOS or non-file-input restrictions; it only bypasses the ≤10-regular-files heuristic.

## Default behavior

The default is `MmapMode::Auto` (`crates/core/flags/lowargs.rs:621-640`). In `crates/core/flags/hiargs.rs`, `Auto` is resolved like this (lines 222-248):

```rust
let maybe = unsafe { grep::searcher::MmapChoice::auto() };
let never = grep::searcher::MmapChoice::never();
match low.mmap {
    MmapMode::Auto => {
        if paths.paths.len() <= 10
            && paths.paths.iter().all(|p| p.is_file())
        {
            // If we're only searching a few paths and all of them
            // are files, then memory maps are probably faster.
            maybe
        } else {
            never
        }
    }
    MmapMode::AlwaysTryMmap => maybe,
    MmapMode::Never => never,
}
```

**The exact default condition is:**

```rust
paths.paths.len() <= 10 && paths.paths.iter().all(|p| p.is_file())
```

Here `paths` is `Vec<PathBuf>` (`hiargs.rs:1055-1067`), so `p.is_file()` is `std::path::Path::is_file`: the path must exist and be a regular file (symlinks are followed). Consequently, mmap is *not* used by default when:

- no path is given and ripgrep falls back to searching `./` (a directory),
- a directory is searched recursively,
- stdin (`-`) is searched,
- any path is missing or is a non-regular file (device, FIFO, etc.),
- more than 10 path arguments are given.

Note the fallback path logic (`hiargs.rs:1092-1135`) inserts either `./` or `-`, both of which fail `is_file()`, so a bare `rg foo` uses no mmap.

## Flags that override it

Both are defined on the `Mmap` flag in `crates/core/flags/defs.rs:4060-4100`:

| Flag | Sets | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `--mmap` | `MmapMode::AlwaysTryMmap` | Always try mmap, ignoring the "≤10 regular files" heuristic |
| `--no-mmap` | `MmapMode::Never` | Never use mmap at all (even for multi-line search, which otherwise reads whole files onto the heap) |

Last one wins when both are given (see `test_mmap` at `defs.rs:4107-4122`). Even `--mmap` cannot force mmap for stdin, streams, or virtual/compressed files — it only says to try whenever mmap is possible. No other flag (e.g. `-u`/`--multiline`) changes `MmapMode`.

In current ripgrep master (`v15.2.0`, commit [`91f5d22`](https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep/tree/91f5d22ef48bde459d90a8c7dbeab416e97d0f2b)), the default is `MmapMode::Auto`, and the exact heuristic is in [`crates/core/flags/hiargs.rs`](https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep/blob/91f5d22ef48bde459d90a8c7dbeab416e97d0f2b/crates/core/flags/hiargs.rs#L234-L248):

```rust
match low.mmap {
    MmapMode::Auto => {
        if paths.paths.len() <= 10
            && paths.paths.iter().all(|p| p.is_file())
        {
            // If we're only searching a few paths and all of them
            // are files, then memory maps are probably faster.
            maybe
        } else {
            never
        }
    }
    MmapMode::AlwaysTryMmap => maybe,
    MmapMode::Never => never,
}
```

So, by default, ripgrep enables memory-map searching only when:

1. **At most 10 search paths** were resolved, and
2. **every one of those paths is a regular file** (`Path::is_file()`).

If no path is given, ripgrep inserts an implicit `./` (or `-` for stdin), which is not a file, so the condition fails and mmap is off. Directories, nonexistent paths, more than 10 explicit files, and stdin likewise fail the condition.

Two caveats: satisfying the heuristic does not guarantee a successful map (it can fail to map, and virtual files/streams cannot be mapped), and the searcher currently refuses memory maps on macOS outright ([`mmap.rs`](https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep/blob/91f5d22ef48bde459d90a8c7dbeab416e97d0f2b/crates/searcher/src/searcher/mmap.rs#L81-L93)).

The flags that override the heuristic are:

- **`--mmap`** → `MmapMode::AlwaysTryMmap`, bypassing the path-count/file heuristic and attempting mmaps wherever possible.
- **`--no-mmap`** → `MmapMode::Never`, disabling memory maps entirely.

If both are given, the last one wins (per the flag tests in `defs.rs`).
